## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design an end-to-end, multi-tenant pipeline with strict isolation, data residency requirements, and cost controls, along with operational concerns such as monitoring and rollback procedures.

## Key Concepts
- **Tenant Isolation Patterns**: Per-tenant resources (topics, buckets, datasets) with dedicated IAM policies
- **Data Residency**: Pub/Sub and Dataflow regionality constraints enforced at deployment
- **Access Control**: Least-privilege IAM with tenant-scoped service accounts
- **Cost Governance**: Quotas, autoscaling limits, and egress monitoring per tenant
- **Operational Concerns**: Auditing, monitoring dashboards, and disaster recovery procedures

## Architecture Components
| Component | Per-Tenant Strategy |
|-----------|---------------------|
| Pub/Sub | Tenant-scoped topics |
| Dataflow | Regional job templates |
| Cloud Storage | Tenant-specific buckets |
| BigQuery | Dedicated datasets per tenant |
| IAM | Granular, tenant-bound service accounts |

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tenant-aware message processing with isolation
function processMessage(msg, tenantId) {
  const body = JSON.parse(msg.data.toString());
  const tenantContext = { tenantId, region: 'us-central1' };

  validateTenantAccess(tenantId);
  const processed = transformEvent(body, tenantContext);

  await writeToGCS(tenantId, processed);
  await writeToBigQuery(tenantId, processed);

  return { success: true, tenantId };
}

// Cost-aware cross-tenant aggregation
async function aggregateAcrossTenants(tenantIds, query) {
  const quotas = await checkQuotas(tenantIds);
  if (!quotas.allowed) throw new QuotaExceededError();

  return await queryBigQuery(tenantIds, query);
}
```

